---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Question from '../components/Question.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Marfa"
  description="Marfa is a shared data layer for your apps. You decide what you keep and how it connects, and every app, script or AI agent you allow works from the same data."
>
  <h1>Introducing Marfa</h1>
  <p>
    Marfa is a shared data layer for your apps. You decide what kinds of things you keep and how they connect, and
    every app, script or AI agent you allow works from the same data.
  </p>
  <p>
    Most apps keep their own copy of your data, in their own shape. With Marfa, an app is a view onto data you already
    own: one writes something, another shows it differently, and you can switch apps or build your own without moving
    anything.
  </p>
  <p>
    You run Marfa yourself, on your Mac or on Railway. It’s free and open source, and WithMarfa’s own apps are native,
    starting with Mac, iPhone and iPad.
  </p>

  <div class="questions">
    <Question question="Can I use it yet?">
      <p>
        Development versions are out now, for anyone happy to run them from the code. Marfa 0.1, the first semi-stable
        release, follows in late October, and the Mac and iOS apps reach TestFlight in November. The
        <a href="/about">About page</a> has the timeline.
      </p>
    </Question>
    <Question question="Where does my data live?">
      <p>
        On your own Marfa, on your Mac or on Railway. Each app asks for the kinds of data it needs, and once you
        approve, it sees those and nothing else. Marfa Cloud will later be an optional way to reach your Marfa from anywhere, and it won’t
        hold your data.
      </p>
    </Question>
    <Question question="Can I build on it?">
      <p>
        Yes. WithMarfa’s apps use the same open API as anything you build. There are Swift and TypeScript SDKs, a
        command-line tool for scripts and agents, and connectors for GitHub, Todoist, Readwise and RSS.
      </p>
    </Question>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .questions {
    margin-top: var(--section);
  }
</style>
